.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-DEPLOY-INFRASTRUCTURE 1 "October 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-deploy-infrastructure \- the older name of eap apply
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap deploy-infrastructure
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-dir | \-d
.IR FOLDER ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-prune ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The same command as
.BR euclid-cli-eap-apply (1),
under the name it had before the two were merged. They used to differ: this
one stored the declaration and had EAP apply it, dropping every setting;
.B apply
created objects from the CLI with their settings but granted no access.
Both now store the folder and have EAP apply it, settings and grants
included.
.PP
One difference from before:
.B \-\-dry\-run
no longer prints the merged document; it asks EAP what applying it would
do.
.B \-\-json
prints EAP's answer as JSON, as this command used to.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap-apply (1)
